            "Give `settle` every run of the test in this invocation (the first run, in a warm batch or alone, and the rerun) and mark the test only when each of them qualifies on its own; otherwise it is a new red and the line says so. Say what a first run inside a warm batch can and cannot tell you about one test's failing lines, and fail closed where it cannot. Tests go through the real shapes: first run bad and rerun listed; first run listed and rerun bad; both listed; first run in a batch.\n\n"
            "**Part 2 (LEAD-134): the words a red-on-main row may use.** The ledger checker (`parseLedger`) allows, in the check column of a row that carries the red-on-main marker, a star (any text inside one check) and the reserved words `ERROR-LINE` and `TIME-OUT`. Each can cover a failure nobody saw on `main`: a star matches other checks of the same test, `ERROR-LINE` lets any error line through, and `TIME-OUT` can never apply to a run that must end normally to be marked. Refuse all three in a row with the marker, with a message that says what to write instead (the exact check as the test prints it). No row that exists today uses them; confirm that and keep the two rows as they are.\n\n"
            "**Part 3 (LEAD-134): the test calls the code it guards.** The test for a log dropped before the rule reads it copies the `{ output, ...run }` strip that `keepRed` does, because `keepRed` lives inside `main()`. If `keepRed` ever dropped the failure record too, the test would not notice. Move `keepRed` (or the part of it that decides what survives) to an exported function the test calls, with no change in what it does.\n\n"
